    "q-140040": {
      "dom_id": "step-q140040",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Determine if the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 9 \\\\ -4 & -4\\end{aligned}]$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}8-\\lambda  & 9 \\\\ -4 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (8-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )-9\\cdot (-4) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -32+36 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $(A-2I)\\text{x}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 9 \\\\ -4 & -4\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 \\\\ -4 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-140040-a",
