    "q-80480": {
      "dom_id": "step-q80480",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Find matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=E,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix and $E=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ -6 & -8\\end{aligned}].$",
      "worked_solution": "We apply the diagonalization algorithm. \n\n**Step 1.** We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix $E$ using its characteristic equation $det(E-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & 9 \\\\ -6 & -8-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(-8-\\lambda )-9\\cdot (-6) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $E$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $E.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E-I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ -6 & -8\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 \\\\ -6 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -9 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ -6 & -8\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 9 \\\\ -6 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 9 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ={R}_{2}+(\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
